WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.580
Scott Kirkpatrick: It’s a huge question
to say ‘What should I write?”

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:11.429
And I do my best to answer that in the easiest
way for new writers in my new book and I try

00:00:11.429 --> 00:00:15.259
to break that down the best I can but I mean
it’s a really big question.

00:00:15.259 --> 00:00:21.040
And the thing I certainly don’t want to
see any screenwriters run into is feeling

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.760
like they have to blindly guess.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:26.330
I try to answer that in the book but if you
don’t have access to that in the book, the

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:29.410
best advice I can give to a screenwriter is…

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:35.120
Larry Wilson: What that story is and it’s
not that cliche to ‘Write what you know’

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.461
because I’ve never written because I’ve
never written a “know.”

00:00:39.461 --> 00:00:49.011
I write fantastical worlds and supernatural
stories but write what you feel and write

00:00:49.011 --> 00:00:52.670
what’s in your heart and write something
that means something to you.

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:53.829
Scott: You can go to a Red Box machine.

00:00:53.829 --> 00:00:57.410
It used to be Blockbuster but those all turned
into cell phone stores.

00:00:57.410 --> 00:01:01.320
Go to the Red Box machines, look at the independent
films on there.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:05.990
So independent would be the family titles
at the bottom, look for the live-action ones

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:11.940
(usually with a dog or an animal on them)
and also look at the big studio films that

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:15.680
are…throughout the machine you’ll see
them in different spots.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.200
Whatever a big studio film is look to the
ones that are right next to that.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.890
Or go on Netflix and find a big studio movie
in the line up and then look at the independent

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:28.250
movie you’ve never heard of that is next
to it, that’s the independent Hollywood

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:29.250
zone.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:33.230
That’s where I work, those are the scripts
that I’m in need of, that’s where I think

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:37.420
you’re going to get your best indicator
as far as genres, the genre themes you’re

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:41.590
going to see, the number of cast members,
all that information.

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:49.540
Larry Wilson: I was a script reader for for
God…probably a decade?

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:54.720
And I was reading seven-eight scripts a week.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:01.060
And the rare one was the great script and
when you found that, that was a very exciting

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:03.560
day at the office.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:10.560
And some times the really terrible scripts,
they were fun because they were so God-awful.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:15.060
The ones that got really exhausting and really
innovative as a script reader who is sitting

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:19.650
there having to read a script, synopsize it,
write a comment about it and then pick up

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:25.950
another, read it, synopsis it…were the ones
who you could tell were written by people

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:34.040
who were looking at what last year’s hit
was and trying to imitate it.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:49.239
Those were deadly and they were safe and bad
and any young writer (any old writer), anyone

00:02:49.239 --> 00:02:55.410
who is just starting to write anything, write
something that you feel, write something that

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:56.410
comes from the heart.

00:02:56.410 --> 00:03:04.790
Scott: I think screenwriters stand their best
chance for really getting noticed by knowing

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:06.819
what the business needs.

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:11.890
So it’s not coming in talking about how
passionate you are about the script.

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:17.860
It’s talking and speaking in a way that
answers to the needs of the production company,

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:22.019
the producer, the studio, etc.

00:03:22.019 --> 00:03:30.010
There are tried and tested genres that are…they
just nail what the business lives on.

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:35.190
And if a young writer wants to come into the
business and just get their emails answered,

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:39.270
get their phone calls returned, it’s being
able to speak about the project and have it

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:40.590
answer to those things.

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:46.270
It’s going to be saying “I’m writing
a family film about this.

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:49.870
I’m writing a film about this and it’s
a very quick point.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:51.530
It’s not a quick point.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:54.540
It’s never a coming-of-age stories, it’s
never dramas.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:58.860
It’s happier films, it’s the kind of films
you see on a Red Box machine.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.330
That’s what Hollywood needs to stay in business.

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:07.080
And so if you’re coming to the table bringing
opportunities for Hollywood to continue staying

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.420
in business, they are going to continue paying
attention to you.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:16.389
Larry: When I decided I was going to be writer
again and I decided I was going to do it with

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:21.309
Michael, in my mind I said (this sounds so
awful maybe) but I said “I’m going to

00:04:21.309 --> 00:04:22.789
write a classic.

00:04:22.789 --> 00:04:25.180
I’m not just going to write another movie.

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:27.939
We are going to create a classic.”

00:04:27.939 --> 00:04:32.490
And who knows what that means and it sounds
obnoxious.

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:48.869
But we knew maybe…20 pages in…that just
something was happening and we weren’t thinking

00:04:48.869 --> 00:04:53.669
about demographics, we weren’t thinking
about who the audiences was going to be.

00:04:53.669 --> 00:05:01.440
We weren’t thinking about an “commercial”
aspects of it.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:05.899
We were thinking what’s good, what’s funny,
what’s making us laugh, what’s moving

00:05:05.899 --> 00:05:13.479
the story forward, what are the zaniest, craziest
ideas we can have.

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:19.460
And it happens with scripts and it happens
in your writing.

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:27.580
There are just times when you feel that you’re
in a zone and that you are so connecting with

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:34.240
yourself and you’re so connect with what
your vision is, that if you can get it on

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.270
a page, other people are going to see it and
feel it too.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:39.710
Scott: At no point am I saying “Don’t
write dramas.

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:41.800
Don’t write comedies.

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:44.529
Don’t write the coming-of-age-story.”

00:05:44.529 --> 00:05:48.619
If you have a fantastic idea for a movie,
I think a writer should hold onto it (just

00:05:48.619 --> 00:05:51.180
hold onto it).

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:56.189
Get the commercial scripts out first, get
a reputation of a screenwriter who can deliver

00:05:56.189 --> 00:06:01.029
what the systems needs, who can write quickly,
who can write well, who can write within budget

00:06:01.029 --> 00:06:05.199
and get a couple of actual paid gigs and you’ll
get them.

00:06:05.199 --> 00:06:08.809
If you go in the opposite way saying I know
what Hollywood wants, they want this…they

00:06:08.809 --> 00:06:14.589
want these very deep dramas, they very deep
coming-of-age dramas and forcing them to do

00:06:14.589 --> 00:06:16.479
that but you’re not going to get noticed.

00:06:16.479 --> 00:06:22.159
But if you come in giving them what they want
which is Tween girl family films or Christmas

00:06:22.159 --> 00:06:28.659
movies with dogs, women-in-peril thrillers,
these kinds of things, Hollywood pays attention

00:06:28.659 --> 00:06:29.659
to you.

00:06:29.659 --> 00:06:35.270
And once you are writing those and getting
noticed for that work and you have a collection

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:38.479
of contacts you can say “I have an interesting
idea.

00:06:38.479 --> 00:06:39.599
Could this work?”

00:06:39.599 --> 00:06:40.689
And then you can present those.

00:06:40.689 --> 00:06:43.749
Larry: People may not think that BEETLEJUICE
is a personal movie.

00:06:43.749 --> 00:06:50.339
It’s an intensely personal movie, it’s
intensely personal for Michael and me.

00:06:50.339 --> 00:06:58.149
And I didn’t know I had a theme until I
had enough work to realize I had a theme and

00:06:58.149 --> 00:07:03.919
almost everything I’ve written are about
broken families who are put back together

00:07:03.919 --> 00:07:19.349
in some bizarre way because I came from a
broken family and so I like to think that

00:07:19.349 --> 00:07:24.009
my work has a sense of art and a sense of
compassion and a sense of humanity no matter

00:07:24.009 --> 00:07:29.720
how bizarre it is, now matter how weird the
world is, no matter how weird the worlds get.

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:34.389
And you’ve got to write something that you
feel and something that you write some passion

00:07:34.389 --> 00:07:36.539
for or just why do it?

00:07:36.539 --> 00:07:40.459
It’s too hard, it’s not an easy job.

00:07:40.459 --> 00:07:44.189
Scott: I would take the high school script
over the foreign exchange one (hands down).

00:07:44.189 --> 00:07:49.219
But I would take it and probably rebrand it
and make it junior high and make it more focused

00:07:49.219 --> 00:07:55.180
on the tween girl audience knowing that nine
to 12-year-old girls are the most likely audience

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:59.119
to be renting movies and buying movies on
something like iTunes, Amazon or some other

00:07:59.119 --> 00:08:05.659
platforms and I would see bigger numbers doing
that and I would make a really nice pink poster…see

00:08:05.659 --> 00:08:06.809
this is how we’re talking.

00:08:06.809 --> 00:08:12.439
You come in with an idea and then we target
an audience we know we need to hit and then

00:08:12.439 --> 00:08:16.929
we create a concept around that and I can
already see the key art in my head and I would

00:08:16.929 --> 00:08:20.739
go talk to my teams about that, I know how
much money we can make Internationally with

00:08:20.739 --> 00:08:21.749
a movie like that.

00:08:21.749 --> 00:08:26.709
I would take that writer and either have them
rewrite it or we’d hire a writer who had

00:08:26.709 --> 00:08:29.930
experience to write the movie we’ve now
just created.

00:08:29.930 --> 00:08:40.550
Larry: I personally imagine saying “Well
this was a hit, so I’m going to write my

00:08:40.550 --> 00:08:42.209
version of that.

00:08:42.209 --> 00:08:47.990
It’s not really what I care about, what
I feel but I can imitate it well-enough.”

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:53.069
I can’t imagine spending a year of your
life doing that but people do it all the time

00:08:53.069 --> 00:08:56.660
and they usually write mediocre scripts.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:01.269
Film Courage: Is being safe something that
has never appealed to you?

00:09:01.269 --> 00:09:07.899
Larry: I don’t know how in my career and
in my life!

00:09:07.899 --> 00:09:10.360
Safe hasn’t factored into it.

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:21.699
And not only the best decisions maybe but
I sort of like jumping off cliffs.

00:09:21.699 --> 00:09:27.000
Scott: Look, comedies are fantastic and anyone
would be correct by saying that comedies are

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:30.120
very popular, high grossing movies, they absolutely
are.

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:37.180
The problem is the best way for a writer to
break into a Hollywood film career is to go

00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:39.770
toward the independent space (not the big
studios).

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:43.410
There’s too much noise at the big studio
level and there’s way too many barriers.

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:46.100
Go to indie Hollywood, that’s where I work.

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:50.360
And that’s where most movies and content,
that’s where it comes from.

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:55.430
So on the comedies front, indie Hollywood
is not just thinking US and Canada, we’re

00:09:55.430 --> 00:09:57.360
not thinking out domestic English speaking
markets.

00:09:57.360 --> 00:09:58.410
We’re thinking global.

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:00.920
We get a lot of our money from overseas.

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:04.610
And the problem with most newbies writers
it the want to write something like AMERICAN

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:10.550
PIE, a really raunchy comedy or they want
to write a really smart comedy with high level

00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:13.060
humor and witty dialogue.

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:15.380
Witty dialogue costs a lot of money to dub.

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:20.209
Social nuances in Russia or India or Japan
aren’t what they are here.

00:10:20.209 --> 00:10:23.670
Not every country in the world celebrates
Thanksgiving and understands what the Super

00:10:23.670 --> 00:10:25.829
Bowl means.

00:10:25.829 --> 00:10:31.850
There are so many subtleties to the international
marketplace that even if you nail your comedy

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:36.689
script and make it wide enough for an English
speaking audience to understand, it’s a

00:10:36.689 --> 00:10:38.089
very limited part of the world.

00:10:38.089 --> 00:10:44.220
And the way a Hollywood executive or the way
a producer would read that is that’s a limited

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:49.079
cross section of money and I’m missing out
on a lot of other revenue that can come in.

00:10:49.079 --> 00:10:52.600
So comedies are great, just keep them in your
back pocket.

00:10:52.600 --> 00:11:00.379
Larry: When things are going well in my writing
and I think it’s true for all writers that

00:11:00.379 --> 00:11:08.529
if you’re a intensely self-conscious for
who is your audience “Is this for the 12

00:11:08.529 --> 00:11:09.529
to 16 year olds?”

00:11:09.529 --> 00:11:14.060
If you’re doing that, you’re not writing
well and you get in a zone and your story

00:11:14.060 --> 00:11:20.509
starts telling you, you’re not telling the
story, it’s emerging from some place within

00:11:20.509 --> 00:11:24.170
you and that’s what BEETLEJUICE was.

00:11:24.170 --> 00:11:30.410
And I used to say to Michael sometimes because
I could feel it going well and I’d say “Michael,

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:31.639
you know we are going to have a toy line!”

00:11:31.639 --> 00:11:32.750
And he’d go “Shut up!

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:33.810
Shut up!

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:35.709
Let’s just finish the script.”

00:11:35.709 --> 00:11:46.110
But it’s a very hard thing to explain…it’s
an epiphany after an epiphany and that you

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:50.050
feel like you’re just onto something bigger
than you.

00:11:50.050 --> 00:11:53.140
Scott: Out of all the spec scripts in Hollywood,
the reason it’s not made it to fruition

00:11:53.140 --> 00:11:55.380
is people writing the wrong thing.

00:11:55.380 --> 00:12:04.680
So my view is this, there’s this stereotype
in Hollywood that every other waiter you meet

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:08.509
has a screenplay on the side, every real estate
agent is writing something on the side and

00:12:08.509 --> 00:12:10.319
in truth that’s reality.

00:12:10.319 --> 00:12:14.939
A lot of people come here with big Hollywood
dreams and want to write that great screenplay.

00:12:14.939 --> 00:12:18.560
And people go “Oh there are not that talented,
they are writing bad scripts.”

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:21.810
Certainly there are a lot of bad scripts but
at the same time there are a lot of very talented

00:12:21.810 --> 00:12:25.749
people who just aren’t writing the right
things.

00:12:25.749 --> 00:12:28.931
They are not writing the right things, they
are not writing what Hollywood needs and they

00:12:28.931 --> 00:12:33.700
are not presenting it in the way it needs
to be presented to get doors to open.

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:37.440
So if they just shift a few of those elements,
if they just write what Hollywood needs (which

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:42.149
are goldmine genres as we outlined in the
book), if they present their ideas in a way

00:12:42.149 --> 00:12:46.180
that Hollywood needs to hear them which is
about pitching your script in a way that “This

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:48.709
is how my script helps your company.

00:12:48.709 --> 00:12:53.720
This is how my script answers that need in
the Hollywood system.”

00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:57.740
And then if you just get yourself out there
at the same time, that’s how you really

00:12:57.740 --> 00:12:58.930
do it.

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:01.680
It’s not selling a one off script, it’s
building a career.

